  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p class="lede">
      I'm a data scientist and ML engineer based in Naples, Florida. My
      background spans neuroscience research, industry data science,
      and government regulatory data.
    </p>

    <h2>What I do</h2>
    <p>
      I build AI and data pipelines that turn messy, high-stakes data
      into clean, usable systems. Recently that's meant LLM and OCR
      pipelines for state regulatory work, and before that, statistical
      modeling and product analytics in industry.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm most comfortable in environments where the data is messy, the
      requirements are unclear, and the output still has to be right.
    </p>

    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>
      I studied neuroscience at Georgia State University (B.S., 2020,
      concentration in Philosophy) and did graduate coursework in data
      science at the University of Colorado Boulder from 2023 to 2025.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before moving into industry I spent several years in neuroscience
      research — first as a research assistant in the GSU Neurophysics
      Lab doing fMRI and EEG analysis, then as lab manager of the
      Perception, Attention &amp; Consciousness Lab at the University
      of Florida. I co-authored a peer-reviewed paper on metacognition
      and causal inference in audiovisual speech (Multisensory Research,
      2023).
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      I default to small, reproducible tools over heavy services. I like
      SQLite more than I should. I'd rather ship a slightly-too-simple
      pipeline I can debug at 11pm than a clever one I can't. The
      research background keeps me honest about how easy it is to fool
      yourself with a number that looks good.
    </p>

